"OverallSummary": "This case involves Richard Mireles, President of Supervision at Independent Financial Group, LLC (IFG), who failed to reasonably supervise a registered representative who was excessively trading in customer accounts between July 2020 and December 2022. The excessive trading violated Regulation BI's Care Obligation (Exchange Act Rule 15l-1(a)(2)(ii)), which requires that a series of recommended transactions not be excessive. Five customers were affected by this excessive trading, paying more than $2.2 million in total trading costs and suffering approximately $2.2 million in realized losses. Two customers were specifically identified: a 77-year-old retiree with a capital preservation objective whose account had a 20.3% cost-to-equity ratio, and an 88-year-old retiree's trust account with a growth and income objective that had a 23.4% cost-to-equity ratio. Multiple supervisors escalated concerns to Mireles about the high volume of solicited trades being placed by the representative, particularly in senior customers' accounts, but Mireles directed only trade-by-trade reviews rather than examining for patterns of excessive trading. For his supervisory failures, Mireles received a four-month suspension in all principal capacities and a $5,000 fine. The Care Obligation violation is evident from the excessive cost-to-equity ratios, which significantly exceeded thresholds that would be suitable for customers with moderate risk tolerance and capital preservation or growth and income objectives."
